WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:09.040
Hello there! In this video, we will&nbsp;
explain why too many dimensions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.000
can be both a blessing and a&nbsp;
curse when doing machine learning.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:20.240
So, it’s Ms. Coffee Bean here with a video&nbsp;
about some important basics in Machine learning.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:29.840
First we have to explain: What are dimensions&nbsp;
and why do we need them in Machine Learning?

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.360
The number of dimensions describes the&nbsp;
number of coordinates we have to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.840
in order to fully localize a point.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.600
For example, we have one dimension if we only need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.760
one coordinate to find the point,&nbsp;
like this point on this line.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:51.120
For two dimensions, we already need&nbsp;
two numbers to localize one point.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.280
Three dimensions are very normal&nbsp;
for us, because space around us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.240
is three dimensional and to localize an&nbsp;
object, we have to specify three points.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:06.800
But the spatial dimensionality in particular,&nbsp;
does not limit dimensionality in general:

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.480
Dimensions can be anything, especially&nbsp;
in machine learning problems.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.520
For example, to characterize&nbsp;
a data point, like a rocket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.720
we might need a dimension&nbsp;
where we specify it’s speed.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:23.520
We can specify the rocket’s&nbsp;
weight in a second dimension,&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.960
it’s height in a third dimension and so on…

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.520
In some Machine Learning contexts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.840
it is quite common to use the word&nbsp;
dimension as a synonym for feature.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:40.400
And theoretically, nothing stops us to increase&nbsp;
the number of dimensions or features to infinity.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.920
We take whatever we can to characterize&nbsp;
a point, because these features can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.840
then used by a machine learning algorithm&nbsp;
to differentiate points one from another.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:55.760
But what do high dimensional&nbsp;
spaces bring with them?

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.840
A large number of dimensions can be a blessing.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:06.400
Think about problems that are not linearly&nbsp;
separable in low dimensions, so cases in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:12.880
which we can not just draw a line to separate&nbsp;
our data with, for example, linear regression.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.040
But certain mappings to higher dimensions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.840
can make the data linearly&nbsp;
separable, so very easy to solve.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:28.960
However helpful in these cases, many&nbsp;
dimensions can quickly become a curse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:35.200
To visualize: We have two points&nbsp;
A, and B in only one dimension.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.360
To compute the distance between the two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:41.840
we just have to compute the difference&nbsp;
between their coordinates. Done.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.840
In two dimensions, the distance&nbsp;
computations become a little more difficult,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.400
since we now have more coordinates for our points.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.280
And if we assume that the points&nbsp;
lie on the diagonal of a cube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:04.160
forming a 45 degree angle with both axes,&nbsp;
then the distance simplifies a little.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.880
It becomes the difference between the&nbsp;
coordinates in one of the dimensions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.440
say x multiplied by the square root of two.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:19.200
And two happens to be the&nbsp;
number of dimensions. Hmm….&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:25.120
We observe the same pattern in one dimension,&nbsp;
where the difference of the coordinates is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:31.360
virtually multiplied by square root of one,&nbsp;
which is again our number of dimensions.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.360
The same holds also for three&nbsp;
dimensions, where the distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.800
between diagonal corner points of the&nbsp;
cube scales with square root of three.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:47.040
And here we generalize, without delivering&nbsp;
the proof now, that the distance between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:53.520
such points A and B is proportional to the&nbsp;
square root of the number of dimensions D.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:00.240
What does this mean? Well, it means&nbsp;
that in a high number of dimensions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:07.600
there is a lot of space to begin with, so&nbsp;
we have a lot of new places to put points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.800
into, since the distance between them&nbsp;
is bigger with each added dimension.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:19.760
When distances increase, it also means that&nbsp;
the overall volume of the space increases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:25.040
so that we need many more points to&nbsp;
sample the space with the same density.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:30.880
For one dimension, it might be enough to sample&nbsp;
only two points, but in two dimensions one needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:38.880
4 points to fill a grid with the same interval&nbsp;
and already 8 points in three dimensions.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:45.004
To be precise, with each dimension, we need&nbsp;
exponentially more points to populate the space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.004 --> 00:04:47.600
with the same density as in a lower space.
Oh no, what do we do?

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.880
High dimensional spaces are like a two-sided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:57.360
coin On the one side, high dimensions&nbsp;
are a blessing for linear separability.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:00.480
On the other side of the coin, there is a curse:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:04.560
many dimensions increase the&nbsp;
overall volume of the space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:11.040
so points and machine learning optimizers can&nbsp;
get lost inside the void of high dimensionality.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:18.080
So what do we do? There are ways to decrease&nbsp;
dimensionality. And in the next video, we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.920
explain how dimensionality reduction works!
Can’t wait!

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:37.840
Are you still here? Go and do not let the dark&nbsp;
side of dimensionality take over. See you, bye!

